  "content": "% Related Work section for Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol paper\n\n\\subsection{Traditional CPI Routing Protocols}\n\nThe concept of Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) extends the foundational ideas of the Physical Internet (PI) by integrating a cyber layer to enhance coordination and control in logistics networks. Traditional CPI routing protocols have primarily focused on optimizing routing decisions based on time or cost metrics. Qu et al. \\cite{b2brouting2023} proposed a framework for B2B e-commerce logistics in CPI networks that integrates fragmented logistics networks using components such as CPI routers, routing tables, and autonomous logistics systems (ALSs). Their research established routing protocols within and among ALSs for making efficient and economical routing decisions. The protocols were designed to optimize either time or cost, with routing tables containing topological information about neighboring routers and networks to facilitate optimal path selection.\n\nWu et al. \\cite{cpilogistics2023} further developed the CPI framework by proposing a two-layer architecture for logistics infrastructure integration. Their CPI-LI2 framework includes a link layer responsible for dividing autonomous areas based on logistics demand distribution and a transport layer that handles routing decisions using real-time data. They introduced Distance Vector Master Paths (DVMP) and Link State Branch Paths (LSBP) routing mechanisms to handle different types of paths within the network. However, these traditional approaches do not adequately incorporate carbon emissions as a primary optimization objective.\n\nIn the context of logistics operations, traditional routing protocols often employ various optimization algorithms. As noted by Qu et al. \\cite{b2brouting2023}, these can be categorized into exact, heuristic, and learning-based algorithms. Exact algorithms aim to find mathematically optimal solutions but often face scalability challenges in large networks. Heuristic approaches, such as genetic algorithms and particle swarm optimization, provide approximate solutions that balance computational efficiency with solution quality. Learning-based methods leverage historical data to make predictions and optimize routes but require substantial training data.\n\nWhile traditional CPI routing protocols have made significant advances in optimizing logistics efficiency, they generally treat environmental considerations as secondary objectives or constraints rather than primary drivers of routing decisions. This approach is increasingly insufficient in the face of growing environmental concerns and regulatory pressures to reduce carbon footprints across supply chains.\n\n\\subsection{Carbon-Aware Routing Approaches}\n\nThe emergence of carbon-aware routing protocols represents a significant shift in logistics network optimization, placing carbon emissions reduction at the forefront of routing objectives. Ng et al. \\cite{carbonaware2023} introduced a carbon-aware routing protocol specifically designed for modular construction logistics. Their approach integrated a unique set of criteria including module weight, travel distance, and carbon emission factors in life cycle assessment to provide routing decisions that minimize environmental impact. The protocol was implemented within a CPI network framework, demonstrating its adaptability to the specific challenges of construction logistics.\n\nCarbon-aware routing extends beyond construction to various logistics domains. Research has shown that incorporating carbon metrics into routing decisions can lead to significant reductions in greenhouse gas emissions while maintaining acceptable service levels \\cite{carbonaware2023}. These approaches typically calculate the carbon footprint of different routes based on factors such as distance, vehicle type, load, and fuel consumption, then select routes that minimize emissions.\n\nSeveral methodological approaches have been proposed for carbon-aware routing. Some researchers employ multi-objective optimization techniques that balance carbon emissions with traditional metrics like cost and time \\cite{cpilogistics2023}. Others develop carbon-specific routing algorithms that prioritize emissions reduction while treating time and cost as constraints. A third approach involves adding carbon-related weights or penalties to traditional routing algorithms, effectively steering routes toward lower-emission options.\n\nThe effectiveness of carbon-aware routing protocols has been demonstrated through both simulation studies and practical implementations. Ng et al. \\cite{carbonaware2023} reported significant reductions in carbon emissions through their protocol compared to traditional approaches. Their numerical studies emphasized the feasibility of the routing algorithm in achieving sustainability goals within the construction industry.\n\nHowever, current carbon-aware routing protocols typically operate with static carbon metrics, calculating emissions based on pre-determined factors rather than adapting to real-time conditions. This limitation restricts their ability to respond to changing traffic patterns, weather conditions, and other dynamic factors that can significantly impact actual carbon emissions during transport.\n\n\\subsection{Traffic-Adaptive Routing Methods}\n\nTraffic-adaptive routing methods represent a critical evolution in logistics network management, enabling routes to dynamically respond to changing road and network conditions. These approaches leverage real-time traffic data from various sources including roadside sensors, GPS systems, mobile devices, and connected vehicles to continuously update routing decisions. The fundamental principle of traffic-adaptive routing is to avoid congestion, reduce travel times, and improve overall network efficiency through responsive route adjustments.\n\nIn conventional logistics networks, traffic-adaptive routing typically employs time-dependent shortest path algorithms that account for predicted or observed travel times across different network segments. These methods may incorporate historical traffic patterns, time-of-day variations, and real-time incident reports to generate routes that minimize delay. Some advanced systems implement predictive routing, anticipating traffic conditions based on historical patterns and scheduled events.\n\nWithin CPI frameworks, traffic-adaptive approaches gain additional capabilities through the integration of cyber and physical layers. Wu et al. \\cite{cpilogistics2023} proposed a CPI routing mechanism that can flexibly select optimal next-hop paths based on real-time conditions, enhancing decision-making efficiency in large-scale networks. Their approach enables quick responses to infrastructure changes and ensures efficient operation even in dynamic environments.\n\nThe benefits of traffic-adaptive routing include reduced travel times, improved delivery reliability, and enhanced resource utilization. By avoiding congested routes, vehicles can maintain more consistent speeds, which typically improves fuel efficiency. However, these methods primarily focus on time optimization, with limited consideration for environmental impact. When traffic-adaptive routing does account for environmental factors, it typically does so indirectly—assuming that reduced congestion and more consistent speeds will naturally lead to lower emissions.\n\nResearch by Qu et al. \\cite{b2brouting2023} demonstrated that routing protocols in CPI networks can effectively adapt to changing network conditions, but their adaptation mechanisms focus primarily on economic and efficiency metrics rather than environmental considerations. This highlights a significant gap in current traffic-adaptive approaches—they lack explicit mechanisms for considering how routing decisions affect carbon emissions under different traffic scenarios.\n\n\\subsection{Research Gap and Proposed Approach}\n\nThe review of existing literature reveals a critical research gap at the intersection of carbon-aware routing and traffic-adaptive methods within CPI networks. Current carbon-aware routing protocols, such as those proposed by Ng et al. \\cite{carbonaware2023}, effectively incorporate carbon metrics but typically use static emission factors that do not account for real-time traffic conditions. Conversely, traffic-adaptive routing methods excel at responding to changing network conditions but rarely optimize explicitly for carbon emissions.\n\nThis gap is particularly significant because traffic conditions substantially impact vehicle emissions. Vehicles in congested traffic experience more frequent acceleration and deceleration cycles, leading to higher fuel consumption and emissions per kilometer traveled. Static carbon-aware routing may direct vehicles through paths that appear optimal based on distance and general emission factors, but become suboptimal when actual traffic conditions are considered. Similarly, traffic-adaptive routing may reduce travel time but inadvertently increase emissions by routing vehicles through areas with driving patterns that generate higher emissions.\n\nThe frameworks developed by Wu et al. \\cite{cpilogistics2023} and Qu et al. \\cite{b2brouting2023} provide valuable foundations for CPI network routing but lack the integrated consideration of both carbon metrics and real-time traffic data. Their respective contributions to autonomous area organization, link layer management, and routing protocols can be extended to address this gap.\n\nTo address these limitations, a carbon-aware adaptive routing protocol is needed that dynamically combines real-time traffic information with carbon emission calculations. Such a protocol would:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Integrate carbon emission factors with real-time traffic data in routing tables\n    \\item Dynamically recalculate optimal routes when significant traffic changes are detected\n    \\item Implement multi-criteria decision making that balances carbon emissions reduction with delivery time constraints\n    \\item Incorporate traffic prediction models to anticipate congestion and proactively adjust routes\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThis approach would require extending the CPI framework to include real-time traffic monitoring capabilities, developing algorithms that can quickly calculate emissions under various traffic scenarios, and establishing decision mechanisms that optimally balance environmental and operational objectives. By addressing these research gaps, the proposed carbon-aware adaptive routing protocol aims to achieve lower total carbon emissions compared to both static carbon-aware routing and traffic-adaptive routing under variable traffic conditions.\n\n\\subsection{Summary}\n\nThe literature review identifies significant advancements in CPI routing protocols, carbon-aware routing approaches, and traffic-adaptive methods. Traditional CPI routing protocols have established foundational frameworks for efficient network operation but prioritize time and cost over environmental considerations. Carbon-aware routing has introduced emissions reduction as a primary objective but typically relies on static emission factors. Traffic-adaptive methods excel at responding to dynamic network conditions but rarely optimize explicitly for carbon emissions.\n\nThe identified research gap—the lack of integration between carbon awareness and traffic adaptivity in routing protocols—presents a significant opportunity for innovation. By developing a carbon-aware adaptive routing protocol that responds to real-time traffic conditions, this research aims to achieve meaningful emissions reductions while maintaining acceptable service levels in CPI networks.",
